#--: What zdata is

#_*"zdata" is the format of a zerolang #_*"data file": a file of values that a
program reads, such as its settings. A data file has the extension #~".zd". It
is written in zerolang's own grammar, and holds only the part of the language
that describes values: numbers, strings, truth values, lists, #~"data" and
#~"choice" values, and names of other values -- with the #~"data" and #~"choice"
types they are built from.

#code language=zdata: """
  # service -- the settings of a network service
  name: "relay"
  port: 8080.u16
  debug: false
  allowed: list { "10.0.0.0/8" "192.168.0.0/16" }
  timeoutSeconds: 2.5
  """

A data file is not compiled into a program. The static data a program carries
(see [#> to="./spec.html#static-data": Static Data]) is written in a source file
(#~".z"); a data file is #_*"read" by a program, while it runs. Today the reader
is the compiler, #~"zc": it reads the project's settings from #~"project.zd" (see
[#> to=./zc.html: zc]). The same file can be checked and printed with
#~"zc data":

#code: """
  $ zc data service.zd
  name: "relay"
  port: 8080.u16
  debug: false
  allowed: list { "10.0.0.0/8" "192.168.0.0/16" }
  timeoutSeconds: 2.5
  """

#--: A data file is a unit

A data file is a #_*"unit", as a source file is (see
[#> to=./units.html: Units]): #~"service.zd" is the unit #~"service", and each
definition in it is a member of that unit. A name in a data file is resolved
as a name in a source file is: the file's own definitions, each enclosing unit,
then the other units. A unit written inside the file (#~"site: unit { ... }")
groups definitions without a type of their own, and a file may keep hidden
subunits in a directory of its own name (#~"service/" beside #~"service.zd").

The units a data file names are found in the file's own directory first, then
in the project's source roots, then in the standard library. They are data files
too: a data file never sees a source file, and a source file never sees a data
file. #~"net.z" and #~"net.zd" may sit side by side; each is its own unit, of its
own kind. The units of the language's prelude (#~"core" and the units it names)
are the one exception: their types, such as #~"text" and #~"u16", are the types
every data file's values have, so a data file cannot be named like one of them.

#--: What a data file holds

Every definition of a data file binds a name to one of these:

#ul:
  [#*: a #b:"literal": a number, untyped (#~"5", #~"2.5") or with its type
    (#~"8080.u16"), a string, or #~"true" or #~"false". A string is a
    #~"text".]
  [#*: a #b:"list", #~"list { ... }", of literals or of other values, with
    labels if it wants them (#~"list { small: 1 large: 9 }"). An empty list
    names its type: #~"list {} out staticlist text".]
  [#*: a #b:"data value", #~"(listener port: 8080)", or a #b:"choice value",
    #~"level.normal" or #~"(tier.paid (seats count: 12))".]
  [#*: a #b:"reference" to another definition, here or in another unit
    (#~"home: site.title").]
  [#*: a #b:"data" or #b:"choice" type, the shape of the values built from
    it: each field with its type, or with a default whose value gives its type.
    A list default is a list (#~"names: list {} out staticlist text").]
  [#*: a #b:"type alias" (#~"texts: staticlist text") or a #b:"unit alias"
    (#~"s: schema"), a second name for a type or a unit.]
  [#*: a #b:"unit", #~"name: unit { ... }", holding more definitions.]

Everything in a data file is public: a program that reads the file reads any of
its definitions.

#code language=zdata: """
  # the shape of one listener, and the two the service opens
  listener: data {
      host: "0.0.0.0"
      port: 80.u16
      tls: false
      names: list {} out staticlist text
  }

  level: choice {
      quiet: null
      normal: null
      verbose: null
  }

  web: (listener port: 8080 names: list { "www" "api" })
  admin: (listener host: "127.0.0.1" port: 9000 tls: true)
  logging: level.normal
  """

A data value has every field of its type: the fields a construction leaves out
take their defaults. #~"zc data" prints each value whole:

#code: """
  $ zc data listeners.zd
  web: (listener host: "0.0.0.0" port: 8080.u16 tls: false names: list { "www" "api" })
  admin: (listener host: "127.0.0.1" port: 9000.u16 tls: true names: list {})
  logging: level.normal
  """

A unit inside the file groups definitions, and a definition may name any
other:

#code language=zdata: """
  site: unit {
      title: "Docs"
      pages: list { "index" "guide" }
  }

  mirrors: unit {
      eu: "https://eu.example.org"
      us: "https://us.example.org"
  }

  home: site.title
  """

A choice arm may carry a value, as a variant's arm does:

#code language=zdata: """
  tier: choice {
      free: null
      paid: seats
  }

  seats: data {
      count: 1
      monthly: true
  }

  team: tier.paid (seats count: 12)
  solo: tier.free
  """

#--: What a data file cannot hold

A data file describes values; it does not compute them or declare behaviour. It
cannot hold a function, any type but #~"data" and #~"choice" (a record, a class,
a variant, a union, a protocol, a facet, a generator, a typedef), an #~"as"
block, a generic unit, a #~"public:" block, a #~"require:" block, an operator
(#~"1 + 2"), an interpolated string, or an #~"if" or #~"match". Each is error
#~"E0041" at its own line, and the file reports nothing else while it holds
one:

#code language=zdata expect=E0041: """
  sum: 1 + 2
  """

#--: Schemas

A file that holds only types is a #_*"schema": the shapes other files build their
values from. A file reaches a schema as it reaches any unit, by its name; the
types are named through it.

#code language=zdata: """
  # schema.zd -- the shapes of a service's settings
  listener: data {
      host: "0.0.0.0"
      port: 80.u16
  }
  """

#code language=zdata check=none: """
  # service.zd -- built from schema.zd's types
  web: (schema.listener port: 8080)
  """

Reach a schema's types with one dot. When a schema sits deeper, in a unit
another unit declares, name it once at the top of the file and read through the
name (see [#> to=./styleguide.html: the style guide], References Through Units):

#code language=zdata check=none: """
  c: toolchain.runtime
  web: (c.listener port: 8080)
  """

#--: Typed files

A file whose whole content is one value of a known type wraps that value in a
construction that names the type. The program that reads the file asks for the
definition by its name and expects that type; the checker has already proved
that the value fits it, every field included.

#code language=zdata check=none: """
  # project.zd -- the settings a program reads as one `settings` value
  s: schema
  settings: (
      s.service
      name: "relay"
      web: (s.listener port: 8080)
  )
  """

#--: Checking

A data file is checked by zerolang's own checker, and every definition is
checked, whether or not anything reads it: a name that names nothing, a value
that does not fit its field, a field a type does not have, is an error where it
is written, with the code and the words the same mistake has in a source file.
A unit the file opens -- a schema, another data file -- is checked as far as the
file reads it, as a program's units are; a mistake in a definition of that unit
the file does not read is reported by that unit's own check.
#~"zc data" reports the errors as #~"zc build" does and exits with status 1.

The tools read data files as they read source files. #~"zl fmt" lays a data file
out as it lays out a source file, and #~"zl lint --full" checks it as #~"zc data"
does (see [#> to=./zl.html: zl]); a definition nothing reads is not reported
unused, since the program that opens the file reads it. #~"zls" checks an open
data file and answers hover and go-to-definition in it. An editor knows the
format by the language id #~"zdata"; the file's media type is
#~"application/vnd.zerolang.zdata".
